     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A visit from the family and wanting things settled before evening rounds aren't clinical reasons for a dose five times the 10 mg maximum. At 50 mg, this 70 kg patient could develop profound hypotension, shock, and pulmonary edema. Amlodipine's long half-life of 30 to 50 hours means those effects would last for days. Giving it once daily would also let the drug accumulate.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 10 mg by mouth once daily**, the maximum approved dose, if the goal is faster blood pressure control. I can add a note asking for a titration plan.\n- **Enter a lower starting dose** (5 mg, or 2.5 mg if the patient is frail) with a note to reassess in 1 to 2 weeks.\n- **Add a second agent**, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide, if 10 mg of amlodipine isn't enough. Tell me the drug and dose and I'll enter it.\n- **Consult pharmacy or cardiology**, if you think this patient needs something beyond standard dosing.\n\nIf you do have a specific clinical reason for 50 mg, such as a documented protocol, it should go through pharmacy verification, not be entered by me. Tell me which of the options above you want and I'll enter it now."
      }
     ],
